{"dachshund":{"id":"c43bda69-139f-447c-bce4-cee0ab0dda40","word":"dachshund","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"breed of dog","tags":["feminine","masculine"],"word":"dachshund"},{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"breed of dog","tags":["feminine","masculine"],"word":"bassê"},{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"breed of dog","tags":["feminine","informal","masculine"],"word":"cão-salsicha m salsichinha"}],"pos":"noun","tags":null,"senses":[{"examples":[{"text":"Say what you will of dachshunds, their peculiar shape makes them the easiest breed of dog to trip over in existence.","ref":"1960, P[elham] G[renville] Wodehouse, chapter XVI, in Jeeves in the Offing, London: Herbert Jenkins, →OCLC:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[21,31]],"id":"74cebd71-5be2-4a47-888f-a8cfd8cf45fe"},{"text":"We had decided to take a small dog for the children and we chose a tan, short-haired, six-week-old male dachshund. We named him Fritz.","ref":"2006, Ted Freeman, Doctor in Vanuatu, Suva, Fiji: Institute of Pacific Studies, University of the South Pacific, →ISBN, page 35:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[104,113]],"id":"9de0b084-9d30-44fa-ad34-3660635c57ad"}],"wikidata":["Q29099"],"senseid":["en:Q29099"],"links":[["breed","breed"],["dog","dog"],["short","short"],["leg","leg"],["long","long"],["trunk","trunk"],["standard","standard"],["miniature","miniature"],["smooth-haired","smooth-haired"],["long-haired","long-haired"],["short-haired","short-haired"]],"synonyms":[{"word":"badger dog"},{"word":"doxy"},{"word":"sausage dog"},{"word":"wiener dog"}],"categories":["English countable nouns","English entries with incorrect language header","English lemmas","English nouns","English nouns with irregular plurals","English terms borrowed from German","English terms derived from German","English terms with quotations","Entries with translation boxes","Mandarin terms with non-redundant manual transliterati