":[["Cyprinidae","Cyprinidae#Translingual"],["freshwater","freshwater"],["North America","North America"]],"raw_glosses":["(US) Any of various related small fish of the family Cyprinidae that live in freshwater and are native to North America."],"glosses":["Any of various related small fish of the family Cyprinidae that live in freshwater and are native to North America."],"tags":["US"],"id":"72d5e037-63c5-40ee-8dd3-7555da993eb1"}],"related":[],"synonyms":[{"word":"dare"},{"word":"dart"}]},"dactyl":{"id":"ccb780a5-7e88-4c65-b5f4-8860ef7fd328","word":"dactyl","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"poetical foot of three syllables","tags":["masculine"],"word":"dátilo"}],"pos":"noun","tags":null,"senses":[{"examples":[{"text":"Now the Bard, glad to get an audience, […] / stuck fast with his first hexameter, / Not one of all whose gouty feet would stir. // But ere the spavin'd dactyls could be spurr'd / Into recitative, in great dismay / Both cherubim and seraphim were heard / To murmur loudly through their long array; […]","ref":"1822 October 15, Quevedo Redivivus [pseudonym; Lord Byron], “The Vision of Judgment”, in The Liberal. Verse and Prose from the South, 2nd edition, volume I, number I, London: […] John Hunt, […], published 1823, →OCLC, stanzas XC–XCI, page 33:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[152,159]],"id":"cac3037b-1fc4-4d78-b2b8-4a688b8a2e02"},{"text":"—My name is absurd too: Malachi Mulligan, two dactyls. But it has a Hellenic ring, hasn't it?","ref":"1922 February, James Joyce, “[Episode 1: Telemachus]”, in Ulysses, Paris: Shakespeare and Company, […], →OCLC, part I [Telemachia], page 4:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[46,53]],"id":"60bbbbd2-2333-4032-898b-f2d13d2969e4"}],"links":[["metrical foot","metrical foot"],["syllable","syllable"]],"glosses":["A metrical foot of three syllables (— ⏑ ⏑), one long followed by two short, or one accented followed by two unaccented."],"categories":["English countable nouns","English doublets","English entries with incorrect language header","English learned borrowings from Latin","English lemmas","English nouns","English terms borrowed from Latin","English terms derived from Ancient Greek","English terms derived from Latin","English terms derived from Semitic languages","English terms derived from a Pre-Greek substrate","English terms with quotations","Entries with translation boxes","Pages with 1 entry","Pages with entries","Terms with Ancient Greek translations","Terms with Armenian translations","Terms with Catalan translations","Terms with Danish translations","Terms with Dutch translations","Terms with Esperanto translations","Terms with Faroese translations","Terms with Finnish translations","Terms with French translations","Terms with German translations","Terms with Greek translations","Terms with Irish translations","Terms with Italian translations","Terms with Japanese translations","Terms with Latin translations","Terms with Occitan translations","Terms with Polish translations","Terms with Portuguese translations","Terms with Russian translations","Terms with Spanish translations","Terms with Swedish translations","Terms with Ukrainian translations","en:Poetry","en:Prosody","en:Three"],"id":"afce7262-6b56-4137-975f-1412675b16fc"}],"related":[{"word":"dactylic"},{"word":"polydactyl"},{"word":"anapest"},{"word":"spondee"},{"word":"finger"}],"synonyms":[]},"dactylology":{"id":"ef15aa68-4e4e-4b63-bd73-f90c870e3b9b","word":"dactylology","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"The use of the fingers and hands to communicate ideas, especially by the deaf","tags":["feminine"],"word":"dactilologia"},{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"The use of the fingers and hands to communicate ideas, especially by the deaf","tags":["feminine"],"word":"datilologia"}],"pos":"noun","tags":null,"senses":[{"links":[["use","use"],["finger","finger"],["hand","hand"],["communicate","communicate"],["idea","idea"],["deaf","deaf"]],"glosses":["The use of the fingers and hands to communicate ideas, especially by the deaf."],"tags":["uncountable"],"categories":["English entries with incorrect language header","English lemmas","English nouns","English uncountable nouns","Entries with translation boxes","Pages with 1 entry","Pages with entries","Terms with Catalan translations","Terms with French translations","Terms with Georgian translations","Terms with Norwegian Bokmål translations","Terms with Norwegian Nynorsk translations","Terms with Portuguese translations","Terms with Spanish translations","en:Language"],"id":"b0d7bcbf-0a58-467b-9c7f-12d35e2e4f49"}],"related":[{"word":"sign language"}],"synonyms":[{"word":"fingerspelling"},{"word":"maniloquism"},{"word":"cheirology"}]},"dacha":{"id":"09432773-f9ce-491e-9ac2-0fba0b40a763","word":"dacha","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"Russian villa or summer house","tags":["feminine"],"word":"dacha"},{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"Russian villa or summer house","tags":["feminine"],"word":"datcha"}],"pos":"noun","tags":null,"senses":[{"examples":[{"text":"The belt is full of dachi, i.e., summer villas, all now closed. […] Most of the dachi are small; but some large, sumptuous, and more securely fenced, a few with iron palisades to insure privacy. […] There are no roads in the environs of Odessa, except among the dachi by the shore.","ref":"1899, Cyprian Cope [pseudonym; James Biddle Baron Cope di Valromita], Arabesques: A Perspective, London: Leonard Smithers and Co, […], pages 214–216:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[20,25],[80,85],[262,267]],"id":"5db51354-34ee-4a18-a413-8dbb31fd171c"},{"text":"This was a great rambling wooden house, fashioned, like most of the Muscovish dachi, or country houses, in the form of a square main building in the centre, flanked by a wing on each side which came forward in a semicircle, like a man’s outstretched arms, as though to embrace the approaching visitors.","ref":"1904, Fred Whishaw, The Tiger of Muscovy, London; New York, N.Y.; Bombay: Longmans, Green, and Co., […], page 295:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[78,83]],"id":"b3cc79bc-ea73-4561-bbbc-7b1ab2d55b0e"},{"text":"Here the dachi are large and sumptuously furnished.","ref":"1953 February 26, “Myth of Classless Society Exposed: Soviet Party Bosses Really Rank Conscious”, in The Vancouver Province, 55th year, number 281, Vancouver, B.C., page 3:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[9,14]],"id":"09772e10-a040-4eb1-a15d-ce204846879a"},{"text":"On the defensive side, Stalin had made equally thorough preparations. Lenin’s personal guard consisted of two men, later increased to four after the attempt on his life by Fanny Kaplan. Stalin’s was several thousand strong, and the NKVD in addition kept other units at a state of readiness. The road to his country dacha, some twenty miles away, was guarded by over 3,000 agents, with cars, telephone connections, etc. When Stalin was actually en route, the road was virtually under martial law.","ref":"1968, Robert Conquest, The Great Terror: Stalin's Purge of the Thirties, Macmillan Company, →LCCN, →OCLC, →OL, pages 546–547:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[315,320]],"id":"d8448357-b0a1-462d-b847-b53cfd659449"},{"text":"The Ambassador warned him of the consequences if his mission to Moscow were a failure, both to Churchill's position at home and to Russia's prospects in the war. He insisted that he should not allow himself to be offended 'by a peasant who didn't know any better'. Churchill listened in silence, then returned to the dacha leaving Clark Kerr outside.","ref":"1986, Martin Kitchen, British Policy Towards the Soviet Union during the Second World War, Palgrave Macmillan, →ISBN, →OCLC, →OL, page 137:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[317,322]],"id":"81e780a0-0b60-49a1-8ad0-c0e9afa3d574"},{"text":"The impact of the city was evidenced by the growing number of summer dachi and rising land values, […]","ref":"1990, Joachim Tauber, Römische Republik und russische Autokratie in der Krise, Peter Lang, →ISBN, page 379:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[69,74]],"id":"4fb3ca42-4315-4b8a-9856-28c0ee4ef244"},{"text":"The villages and holiday dacha settlements that hug the river reflect the divisions that have emerged here: Scattered amid the rundown houses of poor farmers and the modest weekend cottages of factory and office workers are the luxury getaways of those few who have made it big in the new world of “Wild East” capitalism.","ref":"2005 December 5, David Holley, “Ebb and Flow on the Volga”, in Los Angeles Times:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[25,30]],"id":"a37ab917-61e5-4b8e-9123-a6a54406801b"}],"links":[["Russian","Russian"],["villa","villa"],["summer house","summer house"],["countryside","countryside"]],"categories":["English countable nouns","English entries with incorrect language header","English lemmas","English nouns","English nouns with irregular plurals","English terms borrowed from Russian","English terms derived from Russian","English terms with quotations","Entries with translation boxes","Mandarin terms with redundant transliterations","Pages with 3 entries","Pages with entries","Rhymes:English/ætʃə","Rhymes:English/ætʃə/2 syllables","Rhymes:English/ɑːtʃə","Rhymes:English/ɑːtʃə/2 syllables","Russian terms with non-redundant manual transliterations","Terms with Afrikaans translations","Terms with Arabic translations","Terms with Armenian translations","Terms with Azerbaijani translations","Terms with Belarusian translations","Terms with Bulgarian translations","Terms with Buryat translations","Terms with Catalan translations","Terms with Crimean Tatar translations","Terms with Czech translations","Terms with Danish translations","Terms with Dutch translations","Terms with Esperanto translations","Terms with Estonian translations","Terms with Finnish translations","Terms with French tra